      "blocks": {
        "ai_gen_block_0029bb1_JLcCqp": {
          "type": "ai_gen_block_0029bb1",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "The Gemological Institute of America (GIA) diamond carat weight grading scale",
            "text": "<p>Founded in 1931, the Gemological Institute of America (GIA) is a non-profit organisation recognised globally for its work in gem research, education, laboratory analysis, and diamond grading.</p><p>Carat weight is measured with precision to create a consistent way of comparing diamonds by weight. While terminology can vary, diamonds are often grouped into broad size categories such as:</p><p><strong>Melee:</strong> under 0.18 carats</p><p><strong>Small diamonds:</strong> 0.18 to 0.99 carats</p><p><strong>Medium diamonds: </strong>1.00 to 1.99 carats</p><p><strong>Large diamonds: </strong>2.00 to 2.99 carats</p><p><strong>Very large diamonds: </strong>3.00 to 3.99 carats</p><p><strong>Exceptionally large diamonds: </strong>4.00 to 4.99 carats</p><p><strong>Rare diamonds: </strong>5.00 to 10.00 carats</p><p>Although carat weight has a strong influence on a diamond’s visual size, it should never be assessed in isolation. A well-cut diamond can appear more balanced, brilliant, and visually impressive than a heavier diamond with less favourable proportions, which is why carat weight should always be considered alongside cut, colour, and clarity.</p>",
            "heading_size_desktop": 30,
            "text_size_desktop": 18,
            "padding_left_desktop": 380,
            "padding_right_desktop": 380,
            "spacing_desktop": 40,
